{{/*
annotation_comment renders the annotation vocabulary -- "deprecated",
"readOnly", "writeOnly" and "examples" -- as doc comment lines.

A code generator is the consumer these keywords were written for. They constrain
nothing, so there is no verdict to change and no suite case to answer to; what
they carry is meaning for whoever reads the generated type, and the only place
that meaning can go is the comment above the declaration.

"deprecated" is the one with an exact spelling rather than a chosen one. Go's
convention is a paragraph beginning "Deprecated: ", which gopls, staticcheck,
`go doc` and every editor built on them read -- so it is emitted last and with a
blank comment line before it, because a notice that is not its own paragraph is
not the convention and none of those tools sees it.

The context is built in Go rather than here (see mkAnnotationCtxFunc): whether a
paragraph break is needed depends on whether a description was already emitted
above, which the template cannot ask about without repeating the caller's
condition at every call site.
*/}}
{{define "annotation_comment"}}
{{- range .Lines}}
{{$.Indent}}//{{if .}} {{end}}{{.}}
{{- end}}
{{- end}}
